#{[17592186125787 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/c1/c1f4086a1ffb63c41af0a9b50c37a0c837cff641fbf1f79add41757b86a15919.svg)\n\nwith the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3.$ Note that this eigenvalue has multiplicity two in the corresponding characteristic equation.\n\nWe know how to find eigenvectors when all eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic equation (i.e., all roots have multiplicity equal to one). But what can we say about the eigenspace $V_{-3}$ for this particular matrix?\n\nRecall that given a matrix with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ is\n\n![](images/dc/dc954c4b48b2ea3bed7a1ab8370d6a2be9a1e22298e9a838acfe47b5b5d0c47d.svg)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of the matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -11\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}+16{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the system, we obtain the general solution \n\n![](images/a5/a552b978409debb7cf783cd683eceda220ba5eb0f37a82b08add9a66106d75fc.svg)\n\nTherefore, our eigenspace is\n\n![](images/87/8767b25332906b9b6149be3a007cbb89e07d791ed58625e9df369880524f2fdb.svg)\n\nNotice that $s=t=0$ gives the zero vector. On the other hand, the vectors from the general solution form a basis $\\mathcal B$ of our eigenspace:\n\n![](images/06/060bba6a9639bb7658256cea78cf6cb362a3a15a435b3391e806ca43742509ed.svg)\n\nIn particular, we have $\\dim(V_{-3}) =2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125787 #edb/ref 1065 3567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125787 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5d00ddf6-6fe9-56f2-98f7-dd367ce7b91d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125787 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125788 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, given an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A,$ we can have the following two situations:\n\n- If $\\lambda$ is a simple root of the characteristic equation, then the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ has dimension $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{\\lambda}) = 1\n$$\n\n- If $\\lambda$ is *not* a simple root of the characteristic equation (it's a double, triple, quadruple root, etc.), the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ equals the number of free variables in the system of linear equations corresponding to \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The second rule does *not* claim that if an eigenvalue is, say, a double root, then $\\text{dim}(V_{\\lambda}) = 2.$ It says that we have to examine the corresponding linear system and only then make the conclusion depending on the number of free variables.\n\nHowever, there is one thing we can determine about the dimension of the eigenspace, and it is given by the following theorem.\n\n*If an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is a root of multiplicity $k$ in the corresponding characteristic equation, then the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ has dimension not greater than $k \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{\\lambda}) \\leq k\n$$\n\n*\n\nRecall that the multiplicity of an eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic equation is called *algebraic multiplicity*. On the other hand, the dimension of the corresponding eigenspace is called the **geometric multiplicity** of the eigenvalue. Our theorem above states the following: \n\n*The geometric multiplicity of an eigenvalue is always less than or equal to the algebraic multiplicity.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125788 #edb/ref 1065 3568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125788 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "37e113e4-2071-5b13-ad84-c723f4af6af3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125788 #edb/ref 1067 "Dimensions of Eigenspaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A5</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Scalar Multiplication is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A6</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A7</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A8</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1065 4934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "05cdf1b2-a823-5410-8df1-42fa4da19240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1067 "Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1064 "The structure $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is called a **vector space**. However, many other structures possess properties similar to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ These other structures can be called vector spaces too. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn about some important properties used to construct vector spaces. We begin by discussing what it means for a set to be **closed** under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nAs an example, consider the set $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices with real entries:\n\n![](images/a5/a53ec7e9416ae5eee4a1408df358302567a5aa98a393d6ddcb19e861645d2317.svg)\n\nFor this set of matrices, we can define two operations:\n\n- the addition of two matrices, and\n\n- the multiplication of a matrix by a real number (called a scalar).\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under addition**. This means that the sum of *any* two matrices from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under scalar multiplication**. This means that the product of *any* scalar and *any* matrix from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIdentifying that a set is closed under addition and scalar multiplication is an essential first step in determining whether a set can form a vector space." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1065 4935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "18653f19-b4cf-5f0f-ab39-b1ae33ecdc6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1064 "It's important to realize that not all sets are closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Sets not closed under addition and scalar multiplication *cannot* be vector spaces. \n\nFor instance, let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is greater than or equal to $5.$  \n\nWe note that $S$ is not closed under addition. To see why this is the case, let's define the polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ as \n\n$$\np(x) = x^5, \\qquad q(x) = 1-x^5.\n$$\n\nAdding the two polynomials using regular polynomial addition, we get\n\n$$\np(x) + q(x) = x^5 + (1-x^5) = 1\\notin S\n$$\n\nThe sum $p(x) + q(x)$ is not in $S$ because it is a polynomial of degree zero, which is less than $5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that our set $S$ is *not* closed under addition." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1065 6230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b014039e-61b6-58d7-8f35-a72e6153c126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1067 "Sets That Are Not Closed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we discussed several properties exhibited by vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$ and other sets.\nLet's consolidate these properties into a single list.\n\n| **Label** | **Name** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | **Addition is Commutative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A2 | **Addition is Associative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$ |\n| A3 | **Existence of a Unique Zero Element** | For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A4 | **Existence of a Unique Opposite Element** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$ |\n| A5 | **Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A6 | **Scalar Multiplication is Associative** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A7 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A8 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ |\n\nWe'll meet these properties again in a future lesson, where we'll formally define what we mean by a vector space." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1065 6231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "72e9ea87-7b8e-5d00-821a-31813b203a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary of the Addition and Scalar Multiplication Properties of Euclidian Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131497 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131497 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131497 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131497 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131497 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131497 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131497 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131497 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131497 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "878a4e45-5d30-5f1c-aefb-67035a88d122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1045 "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8da487d8-5da0-5730-90f5-0cc952b0b118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134674 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134674 #edb/ref 1061 20246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134674 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f3cce71c-fd0d-5040-949b-813f3fdcbdd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134674 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134675 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134675 #edb/ref 1061 20247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e0f305d-efdf-586e-b799-66fe2b5a8f6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134676 #edb/ref 1061 20248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c20a2e38-a98a-579e-9564-09d78a7f10c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134677 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134677 #edb/ref 1061 20249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134677 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "184ad4b9-e662-5d0f-9c92-14f6b77fcc1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134677 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134678 #edb/ref 1061 20250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134678 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b14c806-2358-52c9-aabe-db4086c3d1ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135049 #edb/ref 1064 "We've already seen how to find the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace in the case when the subspace is defined using an orthogonal basis. In this lesson, we'll learn how to do the same thing in the case of non-orthogonal bases.\n\nFor example, consider the following vectors:\n![](images/28/28ce7470231775d8889555d0dd9f37ee9215044762a40335bd43578b121486a3.svg)\nHow can we find the orthogonal projection $\\mathbf{x}_S$ of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}?$ Notice that since $\\mathbf a_1\\cdot \\mathbf a_2 \\neq 0,$ the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are not orthogonal.\n\nAs usual, we would like to write $\\mathbf{x}$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\in S$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\!\\in S^\\perp.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a5576fcaf9e0be6fc14978eb91dcbaceee75cbfddaa537fdb05d34b8e31e2e0.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp,$ that is, $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\perp S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ into the system to get\n![](images/f1/f1e3c7ae9e966ebcab88901c9e44a5449cb98716f43fc526d306e0af5186923b.svg)\n\nWe can write this system in an equivalent matrix form as follows:\n![](images/47/472c41e1e509d2db9c41dfa95127382eeba7ab5fbaa4172d680c39335694ef2e.svg)\n\nIf we denote \n![](images/26/2650c1020d413a7c988279cc698ab4b7c4caff427615d49b35ef4e9a42651ff0.svg)\nand ![](images/9b/9b50884bd0af404e71f9d7734e9bc054fc9439aba02780cf2beb5fa0a6ac1f32.svg) then we obtain\n\n$$\nA^TA\\mathbf{k} = A^T\\mathbf{x}\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{k}=(A^TA)^{-1}A^T\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nAlso, we note that  $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2$ can be expressed as $\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x}\t= A\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =Ak \\\\  & =A({A}^{T}A)^{-1}{A}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a formula for the orthogonal projection!\n\nNow, we return to our example. Using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Pay attention to the order in which we carried out the multiplication. Carrying out the operations in the order shown above can save us a lot of work!" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186135049 #edb/ref 1065 3626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135049 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a9a954f-9338-5c3f-a9db-9b39a1a60c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135049 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135050 #edb/ref 1064 "If $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is a set of *linearly independent* vectors, then the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $A$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n,$ that is,\n![](images/25/25a85721a3721ace1a32f6ed457749091452df93053d2eba7dbfaa48f6f74189.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The fact that $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ must be linearly independent is important. If the vectors are linearly dependent, we need to find the basis of the span before applying the formula." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135050 #edb/ref 1065 3627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135050 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3274de58-5861-55c3-9614-08540ad4231e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135050 #edb/ref 1067 "The General Formula for the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135051 #edb/ref 1064 "As we've already seen, the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the one-dimensional subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}} \\, \\mathbf{x} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}}{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}} \\: \\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nIf we suppose that $\\mathbf{a}$ denotes the column-vector ![](images/c2/c23a26d5ce1137e222e28a4bc872f9bcffda2cd6430f2d5532618ccf0ed95fa5.svg) then the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is simply $\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a},$ and we can write the formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{(a\\cdot x)}{(a\\cdot a)}\\,a \\\\  & =[\\frac{({a}^{T}x)}{({a}^{T}a)}]a \\\\  & =a[\\frac{({a}^{T}x)}{({a}^{T}a)}] \\\\  & =a[({a}^{T}a)^{-1}{a}^{T}x] \\\\  & =a\\,({a}^{T}a)^{-1}{a}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compare the above with the formula that we use when we want to project onto an arbitrary subspace:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \nAs we can see, they are basically the same thing!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135051 #edb/ref 1065 3628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135051 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "491ffb4a-98e5-59b5-8c01-3d23f872cc3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135051 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Intuition Behind the Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185496 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185496 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185496 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186185497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185496 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7131eaa9-a5b7-5fc9-aee0-c41dbcd479c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185497 #