/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185509 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b91c41cc-51b6-5e81-94b5-47e53eefedb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185510 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185510 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185510 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de2b3a92-bca2-5955-9f57-985d493d01c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185511 #edb/ref 1080 "11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185511 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185511 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1149e63f-1791-5d3e-9b6e-48e6553bdacc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185512 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185512 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185512 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "364e336f-8c62-54e4-a088-dac539c2c563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185513 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185513 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185513 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f3f60e0-b192-58fc-95d2-6460f2bd7ca5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Expected Value of a Discrete Random Variable From a Joint Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188276 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1892c89c-8343-57ff-8c7e-e6edb35bb7c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Expected Value of a Continuous Random Variable From a Joint Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188296 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7447ccfe-83a7-59d4-8386-c74a3ab47b89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing Expected Values From Joint Distributions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186189095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188451 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "35cac34d-08b2-58e8-9601-d836880ca5df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189081 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189081 #edb/ref 1061 34281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189081 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "24560547-2ea6-5ad9-9bbb-ff6eb26fcf18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189081 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189095 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189095 #edb/ref 1061 37912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189095 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c190be0-696c-59a8-9758-3931bd0f72ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189095 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189115 #edb/ref 1061 38102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189115 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "595885fa-5146-5a3a-8643-30f924026039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189115 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189120 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189120 #edb/ref 1061 38236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189120 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "daffc16b-1f95-542f-8a1a-225169b2cb6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189120 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189371 #edb/ref 1064 "To compute the expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ given a joint mass function $f(x,y),$ we must first find its marginal mass function $f_X.$ Once the marginal mass function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]\n= \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n x_i \\, f_X(x_i).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Since we're only interested in the expected value of $X,$ let's compute the row totals only.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.20}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.80}$ |\n\nTherefore, the marginal mass function of $X$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.20$ | $0.80$ |\n|  |  |  |\n\nWe can now compute the expected value of $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.2+1.6 \\\\  & =1.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 1.8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189371 #edb/ref 1065 6253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189371 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0512a52f-ccb2-56a2-b436-f49ec9cb4916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189371 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189382 #edb/ref 1064 "To compute the expected value of a *continuous* random variable $X$ given a joint density function $f(x,y),$ we must first find its marginal density function $f_X.$ Once the marginal density function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]\n= \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty x \\cdot f_X(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189382 #edb/ref 1065 6320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189382 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9ed12512-4300-57a6-b68d-2cacfa2f3488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189382 #edb/ref 1067 "Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448935 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448936 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448937 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243955 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $N$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a|x|,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $f(x)\\in N,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- **II.** For any $f(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- **III.** For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d83ac51c-0c75-5956-b55e-b6c1972a1661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243955 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider $f(x)=|x|\\in N.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot |x|=-|x|\\ne |x|.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)\\ne f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317749 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317749 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317749 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38fc6530-da0e-5ef8-9489-487b09a244dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317854 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317854 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317854 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56588881-20d6-50d9-9ff9-b07f78c8d62a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317966 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317966 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317966 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74eb3b2a-e28b-5f5f-8bad-16800439d2bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318213 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318213 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318213 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c40e8a26-c0ea-5391-8ea7-86af9f58fbcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318281 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318281 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318281 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d79b5d41-92e6-55a5-93af-01f4431ff8c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186317966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7f6e57d-01d6-53e7-aeb3-fe75d269ce0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451231 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186319352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451232 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186319352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451233 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186319352 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319352 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319352 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.$\n\n- **II.** If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=3.$\n\n- **III.** If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186319352 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56ebc46d-299b-5fda-a402-a93bacc04806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319352 #edb/ref 1074 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $3$ pivot columns and $2$ non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319419 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319419 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319419 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0aa598fb-5b9e-5eab-8d75-ba9506c4ca84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319454 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319454 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319454 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15f82c38-57d7-5235-83eb-302196ff1750" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319498 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1